body:
Just came across this issue when trying to execute a migration.

DBAL version v2.10.1
Driver: sqlsrv
Using Azure SQL Server

Steps to reproduce:
(example migration)
```
<?php
declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Migrations;

use Doctrine\DBAL\Schema\Schema;
use Doctrine\Migrations\AbstractMigration;

final class Version20200601000000 extends AbstractMigration
{
    public function up(Schema $schema): void
    {
        $table = $schema->createTable('Order');
        $table->addColumn('testColumn', 'json');
    }

    public function down(Schema $schema): void {}
}
```

Executing the migration with the `--write-sql` results in the following:

```
CREATE TABLE Order (testColumn VARCHAR(MAX) NOT NULL);
EXEC sp_addextendedproperty N'MS_Description', N'(DC2Type:json)', N'SCHEMA', 'dbo', N'TABLE', '[Order]', N'COLUMN', testColumn;
INSERT INTO migration_versions (version, executed_at) VALUES ('20200601000000', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);
```

The `sp_addextendedproperty` stored procedure does not accept the bracket quotes that were added to `[Order]` and when trying to physically execute the migration, throws the error mentioned by @metaturso

Here's a minimal failing test to cover this bug. The problem is isolated inside the `SchemaManager` object (or one of its immediate collaborators.)

> As far as I see this happens with other DBAL versions too, right? [...]

I didn't run it with another version of the DBAL, it's fair to assume the problem exists in newer versions too.

> Because our builds don't contain Microsoft SQL-Server this is hard to fix and protect from regressions.

Neither `pdo_sqlsrv` nor `sqlsrv` are needed, so it should be possible to run this test (or something better) to check for regression.

```php
<?php

use Doctrine\Common\EventManager;
use Doctrine\DBAL\Connection;
use Doctrine\DBAL\Platforms\SQLServerPlatform;
use Doctrine\DBAL\Schema\Schema;
use Doctrine\DBAL\Schema\SQLServerSchemaManager;
use PHPUnit\Framework\TestCase;

class SqlServerExtendedPropertyEscapingBugTest extends TestCase
{
    public function testEscapedTableNameIsNotEscapedInExtendedPropertyQuery()
    {
        $connection = $this->createMock(Connection::class);

        $platform = new SQLServerPlatform();
        $eventManager = new EventManager();
        $schemaManager = new SQLServerSchemaManager($connection, $platform);

        $connection->method('getEventManager')->willReturn($eventManager);
        $connection->method('getSchemaManager')->willReturn($schemaManager);
        $connection->method('getDatabasePlatform')->willReturn($platform);

        $metadataSchemaConfig = $schemaManager->createSchemaConfig();
        $metadataSchemaConfig->setExplicitForeignKeyIndexes(false);

        $schema = new Schema([], [], $metadataSchemaConfig);
        $schema->createTable('`example-table-name`');

        $table = $schema->getTable('example-table-name');
        $table->addColumn('json_data_column', 'array');

        $queries = $platform->getCreateTableSQL($table);
        $extendedPropertyQuery = array_pop($queries);

        $this->assertStringContainsString(
            "N'MS_Description', N'(DC2Type:array)'",
            $extendedPropertyQuery,
            "Unexpected query"
        );

        // This test currently fails as reported in https://github.com/doctrine/orm/issues/7797
        $this->assertStringNotContainsString(
            "N'TABLE', '[example-table-name]'",
            $extendedPropertyQuery,
            "The escaped table name appeared inside of a string"
        );
    }
}
```
